WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.723
At the end of the bakery line there's a packing table, and two belts arrive at it.

2
00:00:07.723 --> 00:00:09.684
One brings loaves from the oven.

3
00:00:09.684 --> 00:00:12.747
The other brings jars of jam from the jam kitchen.

4
00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:14.999
The packer can work in four ways.

5
00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:16.744
Box whatever arrives first.

6
00:00:16.744 --> 00:00:19.135
Finish all the loaves before the jam.

7
00:00:19.135 --> 00:00:22.108
Make gift boxes, one loaf and one jar in each.

8
00:00:22.108 --> 00:00:25.145
Or keep the price board on the wall up to date.

9
00:00:25.267 --> 00:00:27.264
Reactor has an operator for each.

10
00:00:27.264 --> 00:00:33.557
And here's the question that's easy to be shy about: aren't they all just names for, put these together?

11
00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:36.474
They're not, and two questions tell them apart.

12
00:00:36.474 --> 00:00:38.685
First: when is each belt switched on?

13
00:00:38.685 --> 00:00:42.747
Switching a belt on means subscribing to it, signing its order form.

14
00:00:42.747 --> 00:00:44.957
Second: when does something come out?

15
00:00:45.667 --> 00:00:49.249
Let's try the first two, on the kitchen's order screen.

16
00:00:49.249 --> 00:00:54.981
The shop counter sends an order every 30 milliseconds, the website every 40, three each.

17
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.267
First, the packer who boxes whatever comes first.

18
00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:00.529
Both belts are switched on at once.

19
00:01:00.529 --> 00:01:01.499
What comes out?

20
00:01:04.067 --> 00:01:08.933
Orders as they arrive: counter, web, counter, web, counter, web.

21
00:01:08.933 --> 00:01:11.290
Done at about 130 milliseconds.

22
00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:12.279
That's merge.

23
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.941
Now the packer who finishes one belt before she touches the other.

24
00:01:16.941 --> 00:01:19.881
The counter belt runs until its end-of-day bell.

25
00:01:19.881 --> 00:01:21.901
When is the web belt switched on?

26
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:28.994
When the bell rings: the demo printed 92 milliseconds.

27
00:01:28.994 --> 00:01:36.816
Only then does the web belt start its own clock, so its orders land at about 130, 170 and 210.

28
00:01:36.816 --> 00:01:37.981
That's concat.

29
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:43.049
Now make the counter belt endless, like a live feed that never closes.

30
00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:47.221
The bell never rings, so the web belt is never switched on.

31
00:01:47.221 --> 00:01:47.857
Not late.

32
00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:48.281
Never.

33
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.788
It's the customer ahead of you at the till who keeps saying: oh, and one more thing.

34
00:01:53.788 --> 00:01:55.135
You never get served.

35
00:01:55.135 --> 00:01:59.111
So behind an endless source, concat never starts the next one.

36
00:01:59.111 --> 00:02:00.779
For live feeds, use merge.

37
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.334
And the clerk at the exit of the icing room?

38
00:02:04.334 --> 00:02:09.357
That's mergeSequential: both belts on at once, web orders held until the bell.

39
00:02:09.357 --> 00:02:11.483
concat's order, at merge's speed.

40
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.459
Now the gift boxes.

41
00:02:13.459 --> 00:02:15.104
Three loaves, two jars.

42
00:02:15.104 --> 00:02:18.967
The packer takes one of each, boxes them, and repeats.

43
00:02:18.967 --> 00:02:22.400
Then the jam belt rings its bell, and she stops.

44
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:23.973
Loaf 3 is never boxed.

45
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:24.821
That's zip.

46
00:02:24.821 --> 00:02:31.708
It pairs by position, first with first, second with second, and it's done as soon as either belt is done.

47
00:02:32.433 --> 00:02:34.687
You'll meet it most as Mono.zip.

48
00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:44.052
The order page builds a quote from three lookups: a price that takes 200 milliseconds, a delivery fee that takes 100, and a discount.

49
00:02:44.167 --> 00:02:51.953
Mono.zip switches all three on at once, so the quote is ready when the slowest is, at about 200 milliseconds.

50
00:02:51.953 --> 00:02:53.524
That's called fan-out.

51
00:02:53.633 --> 00:02:54.651
Now the trap.

52
00:02:54.651 --> 00:03:01.458
This customer has no discount, so that lookup completes empty: no value, just the bell.

53
00:03:01.458 --> 00:03:03.102
What does the zip do?

54
00:03:03.102 --> 00:03:03.493
Fail?

55
00:03:03.493 --> 00:03:04.432
Leave a gap?

56
00:03:04.432 --> 00:03:05.449
Wait forever?

57
00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:09.023
None of those.

58
00:03:09.023 --> 00:03:13.757
It completes empty, at once, and cancels the lookups still running.

59
00:03:13.757 --> 00:03:18.703
The demo printed: price lookup cancelled, then: Mono.zip result: null.

60
00:03:18.703 --> 00:03:20.964
Here null means completed empty.

61
00:03:20.964 --> 00:03:22.872
No quote, no error, no log.

62
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.384
And here the gift box picture breaks.

63
00:03:25.384 --> 00:03:28.993
A real packer with no jam would shout: we're out of jam!

64
00:03:28.993 --> 00:03:32.087
Mono.zip just turns off the light and goes home.

65
00:03:32.087 --> 00:03:36.147
So decide what empty means for each source, and say it in code.

66
00:03:36.867 --> 00:03:43.595
Where empty has a sensible meaning, like no discount, write defaultIfEmpty: 24.00, 3.50, 0.00.

67
00:03:43.595 --> 00:03:50.896
Where empty is a real problem, write switchIfEmpty with an error, and the demo says: missing discount.

68
00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:58.824
Last, the price board: a cake's weight times the price per kilo.

69
00:03:58.824 --> 00:04:02.933
The weight is 1.5 kilos, then 2 at 300 milliseconds.

70
00:04:02.933 --> 00:04:06.331
The price per kilo is 20, then 22, then 21.

71
00:04:06.433 --> 00:04:08.509
With zip, what does the board show?

72
00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:11.521
Just two prices: 30, then 44.

73
00:04:11.521 --> 00:04:14.609
First with first, second with second.

74
00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:19.701
The board never showed 1.5 times 22, and it never saw the 21.

75
00:04:19.833 --> 00:04:21.005
Now combineLatest.

76
00:04:21.005 --> 00:04:27.191
Once each belt has sent something, every new item goes out with the latest from the other belt.

77
00:04:27.191 --> 00:04:28.493
How many prices now?

78
00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:32.673
Four: 30, 33, 44, 42.

79
00:04:32.673 --> 00:04:35.581
Every state the inputs passed through.

80
00:04:35.581 --> 00:04:38.105
That's what a price board is for.

81
00:04:38.233 --> 00:04:44.918
A zip board would be the politest board in town: it waits for the customer and the owner to take turns.

82
00:04:44.918 --> 00:04:47.059
combineLatest shows every change.

83
00:04:47.059 --> 00:04:48.941
zip shows only matched pairs.

84
00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:51.388
So, by our two questions.

85
00:04:51.388 --> 00:04:54.279
merge: all on at once, out as they arrive.

86
00:04:54.279 --> 00:04:56.964
concat: the next one on after the bell.

87
00:04:56.964 --> 00:04:59.373
zip: all on at once, one from each.

88
00:04:59.373 --> 00:05:02.884
combineLatest: all on at once, out on every change.

89
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:10.220
Here's the safe quote in Java and Kotlin: a missing discount means zero, a missing price is an error.

90
00:05:10.933 --> 00:05:14.030
So, are they all names for, put these together?

91
00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:14.227
No.

92
00:05:14.227 --> 00:05:18.443
Ask when each belt is switched on, and when something comes out.

93
00:05:18.443 --> 00:05:21.605
And with zip, ask: what if a belt arrives empty?

94
00:05:21.733 --> 00:05:22.609
Quick check.

95
00:05:22.609 --> 00:05:31.441
Mono.zip of saving the order, a Mono of Void that completes after 20 milliseconds, and the cake's price, which takes 100.

96
00:05:31.441 --> 00:05:33.849
What does the zip emit, and when?

97
00:05:36.433 --> 00:05:38.788
Nothing, at about 20 milliseconds.

98
00:05:38.788 --> 00:05:44.952
A Mono of Void is always empty, so the zip completes empty, and cancels the price lookup.

99
00:05:44.952 --> 00:05:47.376
Use then instead: save, then price.
